# Line endings: LF, everywhere, declared here rather than left to a
# per-machine git setting, so it travels with the repository -- any clone, on
# Windows or Linux, by a person or an agent, behaves the same.
#
# The property this protects is that a commit followed by a checkout returns
# the file unchanged. The Git-for-Windows default core.autocrlf=true does not
# have it: LF goes into the repository and CRLF comes back out, so a file a
# tool has just written and the same file after a checkout differ in every
# line. That cost Crucible a task hash that reported a change where there was
# none. See ContextWiki, Preferences/Git.md.
#
# The companion .editorconfig asks editors to write LF in the first place,
# which is the part that addresses the cause rather than converting after it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary types are declared, never guessed. Git decides text-vs-binary by
# hunting for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes, and a compressed file can
# carry none at all -- after which line-ending conversion silently corrupts
# it on checkout. A ReportLab upgrade produced exactly such PDFs.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.bmp binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.7z binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
